#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151840 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152091 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ada0f20-331b-5dfa-b203-a0cfa223a587" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Residues of Products"}] :question/id #uuid "af294c61-bf5b-5b8a-a104-853ea1418cb3" :question/math-academy-id "e-14586" :question/problem "Use the multiplicative property of congruences to compute the residue of $76 \\cdot 122 \\textrm{mod} 6$." :question/worked-solution "The multiplicative property of modular congruences states that if\n\n$$\na\\equiv b \\quad \\textrm{and}\\quad c\\equiv d \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nthen, we have\n\n$$\nac\\equiv bd \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nFirst, we note the following:\n$76\\begin{vmatrix}≡12 \\cdot 6 + 4 & (mod6) \\\\ ≡12 \\cdot 0 + 4 & (mod6) \\\\ ≡4 & (mod6) \\\\ 122 & ≡20 \\cdot 6 + 2\\end{vmatrix}(mod6); ≡20 \\cdot 0 + 2\\begin{vmatrix}(mod6) \\\\ ≡2\\end{vmatrix}(mod6)$\n\nTherefore, by the multiplicative property of congruences, we have the following:\n\n$$\n76 \\cdot 122 \\mid ≡4 \\cdot 2\\begin{vmatrix}(mod6) \\\\ ≡8\\end{vmatrix}(mod6); ≡6 + 2\\begin{vmatrix}(mod6) \\\\ ≡0 + 2\\end{vmatrix}(mod6); ≡2 \\mid (mod6)\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n76 \\cdot 122 \\equiv 2 \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,6)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151841 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152092 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "617c7ac8-45eb-5526-93a5-6ef78015c866" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Residues of Exponents"}] :question/id #uuid "043fcc75-20fe-5027-8585-ab24d45ac89d" :question/math-academy-id "e-14587" :question/problem "Use the multiplicative property of congruences to compute the residue of $5^{7} \\textrm{mod} 8$." :question/worked-solution "The multiplicative property of modular congruences states that if\n\n$$\na\\equiv b \\quad \\textrm{and}\\quad c\\equiv d \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nthen, we have\n\n$$\nac\\equiv bd \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nFirst, we decompose the exponent into a product of smaller exponents. For example,\n\n$$\n5^{7} = 5^2\\cdot 5^2 \\cdot 5^3\n$$\n\nNext, we note the following:\n\n$$\n5^{2}\\begin{vmatrix}≡25 & (mod8) \\\\ ≡3 \\cdot 8 + 1 & (mod8) \\\\ ≡3 \\cdot 0 + 1 & (mod8) \\\\ ≡1 & (mod8) \\\\ 5^{3} & ≡5 \\cdot 5^{2}\\end{vmatrix}(mod8); ≡5 \\cdot 1\\begin{vmatrix}(mod8) \\\\ ≡5\\end{vmatrix}(mod8)\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the multiplicative property of congruences, we have the following:\n\n$$\n5^{7} \\mid ≡5^{2} \\cdot 5^{2} \\cdot 5^{3}\\begin{vmatrix}(mod8) \\\\ ≡1 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 5\\end{vmatrix}(mod8); ≡5 \\mid (mod8)\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n5^{7} \\textrm{mod} 8= 5\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151856 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152090 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8c69963-858d-5527-a8dd-33a9e0070f91" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Scalar Multiplication Property"}] :question/id #uuid "aa58ae42-bdf6-5303-9c9c-2e20151f2ed8" :question/math-academy-id "e-14610" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $3x \\equiv 1 \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,20)$?\n\n1. $x \\equiv 5 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,20)$\n2. $x \\equiv 7 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,20)$\n3. $x \\equiv 9 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,20)$" :question/worked-solution "The scalar multiplication properties of congruences state that for $k \\in \\mathbb{Z}$, we have the following:\n\n- $a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad ka \\equiv kb \\:(\\textrm{mod}\\,n)$\n- $a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad ka \\equiv kb \\:(\\textrm{mod}\\,kn)$\n\nThe modulus equals $20$ in all statements, which suggests we'll need to use the first property.\n\nNotice that the coefficient of $x$ is $3$, and\n\n$$\n7\\cdot 3 = 21\n$$\n\nis one larger than the modulus. Therefore, we can reduce the coefficient of $x$ to unity as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3x & ≡1 & (mod20) \\\\ 7 \\cdot 3x & ≡7 \\cdot 1 & (mod20) \\\\ 21x & ≡7 & (mod20) \\\\ x + 20x & ≡7 & (mod20) \\\\ x + 0x & ≡7 & (mod20) \\\\ x & ≡7 & (mod20)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n$3x \\equiv 1 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad x \\equiv 7\\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,20)$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151864 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152093 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "998b6175-c6d6-54a6-a970-003c6c85da7b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Final Digit"}] :question/id #uuid "21d3a5de-63f0-5150-9cb8-c2cbc609bb8b" :question/math-academy-id "e-14651" :question/problem "What is the last digit in the decimal expansion of $17^{13}$?" :question/worked-solution "If $a\\equiv b\\:(\\textrm{mod}\\,n)$ and $c\\equiv d\\:(\\textrm{mod}\\,n)$, then the following properties hold:\n\n- $ac\\equiv bd \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)$\n- $a^k \\equiv b^k \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)$ for any positive integer $k$\n\nTo find the last digit, we need to calculate the following residue:\n$17^{13} \\textrm{mod} 10$\n\nFirst, we note the following:\n\n$$\n17 \\mid ≡10 + 7\\begin{vmatrix}(mod10) \\\\ ≡0 + 7\\end{vmatrix}(mod10); ≡7 \\mid (mod10)\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the exponentiation property, we have the following:\n\n$$\n17^{2}\\begin{vmatrix}≡7^{2} & (mod10) \\\\ ≡49 & (mod10) \\\\ ≡4 \\cdot 10 + 9 & (mod10) \\\\ ≡4 \\cdot 0 + 9 & (mod10) \\\\ ≡9 & (mod10) \\\\ 17^{4} & ≡9^{2}\\end{vmatrix}(mod10); ≡81\\begin{vmatrix}(mod10) \\\\ ≡8 \\cdot 10 + 1\\end{vmatrix}(mod10); ≡8 \\cdot 0 + 1\\begin{vmatrix}(mod10) \\\\ ≡1\\end{vmatrix}(mod10)\n$$\n\nNext, we decompose $17^{13}$ into a product of smaller exponents.\n\n$$\n17^{13} = \\left(17^4\\right)^3\\cdot 17\n$$\n\nBy applying the multiplicative and exponentiation properties, we have the following:\n\n$$\n17^{13} \\mid ≡(17^{4})^{3} \\cdot 17\\begin{vmatrix}(mod10) \\\\ ≡(1)^{3} \\cdot 7\\end{vmatrix}(mod10); ≡1 \\cdot 7\\begin{vmatrix}(mod10) \\\\ ≡7\\end{vmatrix}(mod10)\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n17^{13} \\textrm{mod} 10 = 7\n$$\n\nwhich means that the last digit of $17^{13}$ is $7$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152483 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152091 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ada0f20-331b-5dfa-b203-a0cfa223a587" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Residues of Products"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "fdf909e6-768e-5c7e-aada-e39202d1e154" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149472}] :question/id #uuid "b81b6d2b-abd2-5154-9eb2-356526df808d" :question/math-academy-id "q-213022" :question/problem "Use the multiplicative property of congruences to compute the following residue.$84 \\cdot 102mod5 =$\n{{field-1}}"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152486 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152093 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "998b6175-c6d6-54a6-a970-003c6c85da7b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Final Digit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb347cc9-4a45-5b47-be43-00d3b07d7d20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149486} {:answer/id #uuid "0465179b-e1ec-5b01-8a6a-5e4ce4f73c5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149487} {:answer/id #uuid "08290b50-040d-51ed-b051-a6577e32cf78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149488} {:answer/id #uuid "38ae6e4b-07bd-5b5d-810f-f8bb8d33eb96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149489} {:answer/id #uuid "3f126979-8756-501f-b118-edf24f7e51fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149490}] :answer-field/id #uuid "45f61d56-edf4-5187-95ae-2ba7267c53c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149485}] :question/id #uuid "73829fe7-6343-5529-8a3e-7f6982fe6b1b" :question/math-academy-id "q-213082" :question/problem "What is the last digit in the decimal expansion of $19^{19}$?"}]}
